Orchestrator-directed change (observed 03:49Z): years 2013 and 2014 archived
using per-chapter singles (HS2_BATCH_SIZE effectively 1) after batches of
25/20/10 chapters proved unreliable at the default 60s timeout; imports
2013 in fact succeeded cleanly with 4 clean 25-chapter batches, and the
"failures" were ReadTimeouts on large-but-legitimate responses (one batch
observed to succeed at 53.6s, another at 59.8s, both near the old 60s
ceiling), not rejections. Per orchestrator instruction: pipeline/acquire.py
now retries HS2_BATCH_SIZE=25 chapter batches with a raised
HS2_BATCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=180 (same 3 bounded retries), falling back to
per-chapter (60s each) only on a batch that still fails at 180s. If two
25-chapter batches in a row fail even at 180s, HS2 batching is disabled for
the remainder of this acquisition (state['hs2_batches_disabled']) and every
subsequent chapter, year, and flow is fetched one at a time; this is a
one-way switch, logged here if it triggers. census_client.py's
CensusClient.fetch() gained an optional timeout_seconds parameter
(default unchanged at 60s) to support this without touching any other
caller's behavior. Years 2015 and earlier are unaffected (already archived
under the per-chapter strategy); this change applies from year 2016
onward. The fingerprint-before reading and the completed availability
probes are untouched; only the fingerprint-after read (at the very end of
acquisition, after all configured years are archived) remains pending.
